WEBVTT

00:00.451 --> 00:02.625
- [Voiceover] A lot of
what geometry is about

00:02.625 --> 00:05.003
is proving things about the world.

00:05.003 --> 00:08.477
And in order to really prove
things about the world,

00:08.477 --> 00:11.036
we have to be very careful, very precise,

00:11.036 --> 00:12.702
very exact with our language

00:12.702 --> 00:14.064
so that we know what we're proving

00:14.064 --> 00:16.056
and we know what we're assuming

00:16.056 --> 00:19.104
and what type of deductions we are making

00:19.104 --> 00:20.871
as we prove things.

00:20.871 --> 00:23.450
And so, to get some practice being precise

00:23.450 --> 00:25.177
and exact with our language,

00:25.177 --> 00:27.087
I'm going to go through some exercises

00:27.087 --> 00:31.663
from the Geometric Definitions Exercise

00:31.663 --> 00:34.038
on Khan Academy.

00:34.038 --> 00:36.688
So this first one says,
"Three students attempt

00:36.688 --> 00:39.817
"to define what it means for lines l and m

00:39.817 --> 00:41.727
"to be perpendicular.

00:41.727 --> 00:45.201
"Can you match the teacher's
comments to the definitions?"

00:45.201 --> 00:47.030
All right. So looks like
three different students

00:47.030 --> 00:50.505
attempt definitions of what
it means to be perpendicular,

00:50.505 --> 00:52.178
and then there's these teacher's comments

00:52.178 --> 00:54.088
that we can move around.

00:54.088 --> 00:56.465
So we're gonna, I guess,
pretend that we're the teacher.

00:56.465 --> 00:59.553
So Ruby's definition
for being perpendicular:

00:59.553 --> 01:02.926
"l and m," lines l and
m, "are perpendicular

01:02.926 --> 01:04.876
"if they never meet."

01:04.876 --> 01:06.176
Well, that's not true.

01:06.176 --> 01:09.874
In fact, perpendicular lines
for sure will intersect.

01:09.874 --> 01:12.455
So, in fact, they
intersect at right angles.

01:12.455 --> 01:14.181
So that is not going to be,

01:14.181 --> 01:15.791
that's not going to be correct.

01:15.791 --> 01:17.071
And so, actually this looks right.

01:17.071 --> 01:18.716
"Were you thinking of parallel lines?"

01:18.716 --> 01:21.581
Because that's looks like
what she was trying to define.

01:21.581 --> 01:24.994
If things are on the same
plane and they never intersect,

01:24.994 --> 01:28.164
then you are talking about parallel lines.

01:28.164 --> 01:30.399
I also got Shriya's definition.

01:30.399 --> 01:33.995
"l and m are perpendicular
if the meet at one point

01:33.995 --> 01:36.839
"and one of the angles at
their point of intersection

01:36.839 --> 01:39.948
"is a right angle."
Well, that seems spot on.

01:39.948 --> 01:40.842
So let's see.

01:40.842 --> 01:43.158
I would say, "Woohoo! Nice Work!

01:43.158 --> 01:44.986
"I couldn't have said it better myself."

01:44.986 --> 01:46.490
Now let's just make sure this comment

01:46.490 --> 01:48.257
matches for this definition.

01:48.257 --> 01:51.630
Abhishek says, "l and m are perpendicular

01:51.630 --> 01:53.885
"if they meet at a single point,

01:53.885 --> 01:57.523
"such that the two lines make a 'T'."

01:57.523 --> 02:01.423
Well, that's, in a hand-wavy
way, kind of right.

02:01.423 --> 02:04.157
When you imagine perpendicular lines,

02:04.157 --> 02:05.782
you could imagine them kind of forming

02:05.782 --> 02:08.484
a cross or, I guess, part of ...

02:08.484 --> 02:10.468
You know, a T would be part of it.

02:10.468 --> 02:12.731
But I think this comment is spot-on.

02:12.731 --> 02:15.435
The teacher's comment, "Your
definition is kind of correct,

02:15.435 --> 02:17.670
"but it lacks mathematical precision."

02:17.670 --> 02:19.844
You know, what does he mean by a T?

02:19.844 --> 02:23.074
What does it mean to "make a T"?

02:23.074 --> 02:25.207
Shriya's definition is much more precise.

02:25.207 --> 02:26.711
They're perpendicular "if
they meet at one point

02:26.711 --> 02:29.311
"and one of their angles at
their point of intersection

02:29.311 --> 02:32.461
"is a right angle," is a 90 degree angle.

02:32.461 --> 02:33.923
Let's check our answers.

02:33.923 --> 02:34.980
Let's do a few more of these.

02:34.980 --> 02:36.524
This is actually a lot of fun.

02:36.524 --> 02:38.474
So once again, we're
gonna have three students

02:38.474 --> 02:40.465
attempting to define, but
now they're going to define

02:40.465 --> 02:43.025
"an object called an angle."

02:43.025 --> 02:45.870
"Can you match the teacher's
comments to the definitions?"

02:45.870 --> 02:48.166
So Ruby, oh, it's three,
same three students.

02:48.166 --> 02:52.574
Ruby says, "The amount of turn
between two straight lines

02:52.574 --> 02:56.272
"that have a common vertex."

02:56.272 --> 02:59.482
Well, this is kind of getting there.

02:59.482 --> 03:02.388
The definition of an angle,
we typically talk about

03:02.388 --> 03:05.720
two rays with a common vertex.

03:05.720 --> 03:09.072
She's talking about two
lines with a common vertex.

03:09.072 --> 03:11.815
And she's talking about
the amount of turn.

03:11.815 --> 03:13.359
So she's really talk
about more of, kind of,

03:13.359 --> 03:15.167
the measure of an angle.

03:15.167 --> 03:17.381
So let's see what comment here.

03:17.381 --> 03:19.076
So, "You seem to be getting at the idea

03:19.076 --> 03:20.275
"of the measure of an angle,

03:20.275 --> 03:21.720
and "not the definition
of the angle itself."

03:21.720 --> 03:22.482
So this is actually right.

03:22.482 --> 03:23.661
I would put this one right here.

03:23.661 --> 03:24.821
We just got lucky.

03:24.821 --> 03:26.504
This was already aligned.

03:26.504 --> 03:30.143
So Shriya's definition: "Two
lines that come together."

03:30.143 --> 03:32.224
So, once again, this is kind of ...

03:32.224 --> 03:37.192
The definition of an angle is
two rays with a common vertex.

03:37.192 --> 03:38.706
So, "Two lines that come together,"

03:38.706 --> 03:40.168
this is just intersecting lines.

03:40.168 --> 03:42.647
Now, when that happens, you
might be forming some angles,

03:42.647 --> 03:44.983
but I would just say, "Were you thinking

03:44.983 --> 03:46.894
of intersecting lines?"

03:46.894 --> 03:48.946
And let's see what Abhishek says.

03:48.946 --> 03:52.744
"A figure composed of two rays
sharing a common endpoint.

03:52.744 --> 03:55.183
"The common endpoint is
known as the vertex."

03:55.183 --> 03:59.612
Yup. That's a good definition of an angle.

03:59.612 --> 04:01.927
So Abhishek got it this time.

04:01.927 --> 04:03.269
Let's do another one.

04:03.269 --> 04:05.645
So three students are
now attempting to define

04:05.645 --> 04:08.347
"what it means for two
lines to be parallel."

04:08.347 --> 04:10.196
So now let's match the teacher's comments.

04:10.196 --> 04:12.615
So Daniela says, "Two lines are parallel

04:12.615 --> 04:16.353
"if they are distinct
and one can be translated

04:16.353 --> 04:19.402
"on top of the other."

04:19.402 --> 04:20.817
All right.

04:20.817 --> 04:24.007
So that actually seems pretty interesting.

04:24.007 --> 04:24.983
That's actually not the first way

04:24.983 --> 04:26.792
that I would have defined parallel lines.

04:26.792 --> 04:28.639
I would have said, "Hey, if
they're on the same plane

04:28.639 --> 04:31.485
"and they don't intersect,
then they are parallel."

04:31.485 --> 04:32.987
But this is, this seems pretty good

04:32.987 --> 04:35.161
because, if you're translating something,

04:35.161 --> 04:39.285
you're not, you aren't going to rotate it,

04:39.285 --> 04:41.056
you're not going to change its direction,

04:41.056 --> 04:42.520
I guess, one way to think about it.

04:42.520 --> 04:43.982
And so, if you're translating one ...

04:43.982 --> 04:44.856
If you can trans-

04:44.856 --> 04:46.359
If they're two different lines,

04:46.359 --> 04:50.077
but you can shift them without
changing their direction,

04:50.077 --> 04:51.704
which is what translation is all about,

04:51.704 --> 04:54.324
on top of each other, that
actually feels pretty good.

04:54.324 --> 04:57.323
So, I'll put that right over there.

04:57.323 --> 05:00.044
So Ori says, "Two lines are parallel

05:00.044 --> 05:03.080
"if they are close together
but don't intersect."

05:03.896 --> 05:06.843
So, if you're trying to
define parallel lines,

05:06.843 --> 05:08.203
parallel lines, it doesn't matter

05:08.203 --> 05:10.905
if they're close together or not.

05:10.905 --> 05:12.074
It's, they just have to be

05:12.074 --> 05:13.751
in the same plane and not intersect.

05:13.751 --> 05:17.144
They can be very far apart and
they could still be parallel.

05:17.144 --> 05:19.399
So this isn't an incorrect statement.

05:19.399 --> 05:21.349
You could have two lines
that are close together

05:21.349 --> 05:22.995
and don't intersect on the same plane,

05:22.995 --> 05:25.173
and they are going to be parallel.

05:25.173 --> 05:26.635
But this isn't a good definition,

05:26.635 --> 05:28.222
because you can also have parallel lines

05:28.222 --> 05:29.725
that are far apart.

05:29.725 --> 05:32.791
And so, actually I'd go with
this statement right here:

05:32.791 --> 05:34.112
"Part of your definition is correct,

05:34.112 --> 05:35.230
"but the other part is not.

05:35.230 --> 05:37.952
"Parallel lines don't have
to be close together."

05:37.952 --> 05:39.882
So this isn't a good
definition of parallel lines.

05:39.882 --> 05:42.423
So let's see. Kaori:
"Two lines are parallel

05:42.423 --> 05:44.839
"as long as they aren't perpendicular."

05:44.839 --> 05:46.242
Well, that's just not true,

05:46.242 --> 05:47.846
because you can intersect,

05:47.846 --> 05:49.492
you can have two lines that intersect

05:49.492 --> 05:52.336
at non-right angles, and
they're not parallel,

05:52.336 --> 05:54.185
and they're also not perpendicular.

05:54.185 --> 05:57.071
So this is, you know, "Sorry,
your definition is incorrect."

05:57.071 --> 05:58.025
This is actually a lot of fun,

05:58.025 --> 05:59.488
pretending to be the teacher.

05:59.488 --> 06:01.621
Let's do another one.

06:02.682 --> 06:04.084
All right.

06:04.084 --> 06:06.398
So "Three students attempt to define

06:06.398 --> 06:08.653
"what a line segment is."

06:08.653 --> 06:12.205
And we have a depiction of a
line segment right over here.

06:12.205 --> 06:15.036
We have point P, point
Q, and the line segment

06:15.036 --> 06:18.307
is all the points in between P and Q.

06:18.307 --> 06:22.732
So, so let's match the teacher's comments

06:22.732 --> 06:24.154
to the definitions.

06:24.154 --> 06:26.788
Ivy's definition: "All of the points

06:26.788 --> 06:30.331
"in line with P and Q,
extending infinitely

06:30.331 --> 06:32.098
"in both directions."

06:32.098 --> 06:34.089
Well, that would be the
definition of a line.

06:34.089 --> 06:35.786
That would be the line P, Q.

06:35.786 --> 06:37.412
That would be, if you're
extending infinitely

06:37.412 --> 06:39.999
in both directions, so ...

06:40.573 --> 06:41.966
I would say, "Are you thinking of a line

06:41.966 --> 06:44.237
"instead of a line segment?"

06:44.237 --> 06:47.691
Ethan's definition: "The
exact distance from P to Q.

06:47.691 --> 06:48.576
Well, that's just a ...

06:48.576 --> 06:52.195
that's the length of a line segment.

06:52.195 --> 06:54.957
That's not exactly what a line segment is.

06:54.957 --> 06:57.030
And see, Ebuka's definition.

06:57.030 --> 07:00.401
"The points P and Q, which
are called endpoints,

07:00.401 --> 07:02.067
"and all of the points in a straight line

07:02.067 --> 07:03.895
"between points P and Q.

07:03.895 --> 07:06.150
Yep. That looks like a good definition

07:06.150 --> 07:08.914
for a line segment.

07:08.914 --> 07:10.762
So we can just check our,

07:10.762 --> 07:12.536
we can just check our answer.

07:13.658 --> 07:14.776
So, looking good.

07:14.776 --> 07:15.893
Let's do one more of this.

07:15.893 --> 07:18.612
I'm just really enjoying
pretending to be a teacher.

07:19.348 --> 07:21.217
All right. "Three
students attempt to define

07:21.217 --> 07:23.046
"what a circle is."

07:23.046 --> 07:24.427
Define what a circle is.

07:24.427 --> 07:26.114
"Can you match the teacher's
comments to the definitions?"

07:26.114 --> 07:29.060
Duru. "The set of all points in a plane

07:29.060 --> 07:32.087
"that are the same distance
away from some given point,

07:32.087 --> 07:33.794
"which we call the center."

07:33.794 --> 07:36.516
That just seems like a pretty
good definition of a circle.

07:36.516 --> 07:39.299
So, I would, you know,
"Stupendous! Well done."

07:39.299 --> 07:41.656
Oliver's definition.

07:41.656 --> 07:44.704
"The set of all points in 3D space

07:44.704 --> 07:47.489
"that are the same distance
from a center point."

07:47.489 --> 07:48.971
If we're talking about 3D space

07:48.971 --> 07:50.880
and the set of all points
that are equidistant

07:50.880 --> 07:52.404
from that point in 3D space,

07:52.404 --> 07:55.167
now we're talking about
a sphere, not a circle.

07:55.167 --> 07:57.546
And so, "You seem to be confusing

07:57.546 --> 07:59.557
"a circle with a sphere."

07:59.557 --> 08:03.275
And then, finally, "A
perfectly round shape."

08:03.275 --> 08:06.118
Well, that's kind of true.

08:06.118 --> 08:07.703
But if you're talk about three dimensions,

08:07.703 --> 08:09.267
you could be talking about a sphere.

08:09.267 --> 08:11.096
If you're talking about, if
you go beyond three dimensions,

08:11.096 --> 08:12.802
hypersphere, whatever else.

08:12.802 --> 08:14.875
In two dimensions, yeah,
perfectly round shape,

08:14.875 --> 08:16.683
most people would call it a circle.

08:16.683 --> 08:18.572
But that doesn't have a
lot of precision to it.

08:18.572 --> 08:20.238
It doesn't have, it doesn't give us a lot

08:20.238 --> 08:23.062
that we can work with from a
mathematical point of view.

08:23.062 --> 08:25.094
So I would say, actually,
what the teacher's saying:

08:25.094 --> 08:27.634
"Your definition needs
to be much more precise."

08:27.634 --> 08:30.742
Duru's definition is
much, much more precise.

08:30.742 --> 08:34.143
The set of all points that
are equidistant from ...

08:34.143 --> 08:36.228
in a plane, that are equidistant away

08:36.228 --> 08:38.648
from a given point,
which we call the center.

08:38.648 --> 08:42.451
So yep. Carlos could use a
little bit more precision.

08:43.594 --> 08:45.072
We're all done.
